A||N||12||Seanh||Call me stupid, but I still cant find my coolant leak.||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-08-2000||10:54 AM||||I have all day today to work on car. I have the batt. out, and I am all prepared to remove the water pump. Once I got everthing out, I start looking at the engine, and now I dont think it is my water pump or it gasket/seal. I am not a very good machanic, but Ill try and explain. Okay, theres that coolant hose that comes from a pipe infront of the engine and goes to the thermostat housing. I remove the thero and that hose and noticed a smaller hose going in underneath the thermo. I am having a hell of a time getting it off from there. (Yes, I did remove the hose clamps) Right where the thermo housing connects to the top of the engine, there is what looks like old coolant there. Also, right under where the thermo housing connects to the engine, there is a sensor, I would think it is the coolant sensor, but I dont know for sure. In front of it (I mean twords the front of the car) there is alot of grim, fairly moist, and I think coolant has leaked there also. When I first got the car home after overheating, I looked under the car, and it looked like coolant was running off the top of the A/C compressor. So I figured it was the metal pipe infront of the engine that has a tendancy to rust out. I dont think that is what happend looking at it now. Also, another poss. is the weep hole, but I dont think coolant would be on top of the water pump and near the thermo housing if it was the weep hole, right? I think that is probally ruled out. Could it be the seal/gasket around the water pump? It doesn't look like a leak around the water pump would get coolant the far away. What does it sound like to you? I dont have any way to pressurize the cooling system. Could I just pour some water down the thermo housing with the thermo removed and fill it up, and wait to see if it starts dripping from anywhere? If it does, then I have found my leak, if not, then that either says there is not enought pressure on it, or my leak is after the water pump, or the pump itself. Please help, I am starting my miss driving my baby. I dont know what I am going to do when I go to rebuild the car in Jan. Maybe buy another Fiero?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000001||JetroGT||10-09-2000||08:21 AM||||I would refill the over flow, "puke tank" and let it sit and idle untill you see a leak. That will give you a general idea of where the leak is. I hope that helps. Don't burn your self!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2000||09:06 AM||||Seanh,<BR>I agree with you about coolant being on top of the water pump not being the weep hole.<BR>Double check condition ALL hoses for swelling and cracks, if any are found, replace them. Look at the thermostate housilg its self looking for rust at seams and heavy corrosion where coolant lines attach<BR>Food for thought: have you replaced the thermostate lately? You just may have a bad cap or it had something under the lip not allowing for a good seal.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fldevel||10-09-2000||04:31 PM||||i had this problem once also .. i tore almost all the hoses off the front of that thing till i found a tiny little crack barly visible on the top of the watter pump, it would never leak unless the car warmed up and had pressure in the pump ..then it would spurt watter everywhare ... hope this helps some .. later ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||10-09-2000||05:11 PM||||Ok. I have a problem. I broke a small vacum (sp?) line. Can I just tape it together really good until I can replace it? It is on the EGR system, so I think the car shold run okay without it for a while. I have the back of the car up on jacks, would the system still pressurize correctly? Im sure it would, but just making sure. No, I havent replaced the thermostat at all, I havent even removed the cap ever. I will clean it all off and fill it up, and run the car. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000005||kinboyatuwo||10-10-2000||12:16 PM||||I searched all week lonog last year for a mysterious leak and found it to be a frost plug, hey they are hard to find.  Check these , mine rotted out and was really cheap to replace<P><BR>hope this helps.<BR>kinboyatuwo@yahoo.com<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000006||Seanh||10-10-2000||10:06 PM||||Hey guys, I worked on the car today. I had to replace the coolant fan switch. I got it fixed up. I put the rest of the engine back together and filled up the overflow tank. I fired it up and according to the engine temp guage inside, it got up to operating temp in about 4 min, started overheating and getting hotter. The fan did not come on, nor could I feel any heat from any coolant hoses. I am wondering if I did somthing wrong. If I am not mistaken, shouldnt I have filled the radiator also? I am sure my system is probally all leaked out. Anyway, any ideas? Thanks.<BR>BTW, it did not leak any coolant at all. nor did any water leave the puke tank.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 10-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||10-11-2000||09:37 AM||||Seanh,<P>It doesn't take a very large air bubble to air lock the water pump.  Try to purge the system again.  It often takes several tries.<P>With the car level or in a slight nose down (not too steep) position remove both the radiator cap and the t-stat cap.  Fill the radiator.  Now fill from the rear until you see coolant starting to come out of the radiator.  Shake the car to get air bubbles moving.<P>Replace the radiator cap and continue to add coolant to the T-stat housing.  Do this slowly so the air bubbles can travel to the top of the system and escape.<P>Start the engine without the t-stat or its cover and let it run for a few seconds to see that coolant is circulating and to see if any more air escapes.  Shut down the engine, top off the coolant, install tha T-stat and button it up.<P>If the coolant is low the temp gauge will swing from way hot to cold as the air moves around the system.  I've had as little as about a pint to a quart of coolant make the difference between cool running and overheating.<P>When an air bubble hits the water pump it loses prime and stops pumping coolant for a short while.  It takes very little time with no coolant flow to show overheat conditions.<P>Always be very carefull when opening either the radiator cap or the T-stat housing cap.  It does not take much pressure in the system to cause a spray of steam and hot coolant.  <P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000008||EdsB52||10-11-2000||10:19 AM||||Stupid  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. You said to call you that. Why don't don't you obtain one of those coolant system pressurizers that hook up to the radiator cap, warm the engine, turn the engine off, install the tool, and pressureize while you inspect for leaks. I've got a snap-on coolant system tester that works great. I haven't tried it, but you might luck out and and have it work on the thermostat access cap.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||10-11-2000||05:10 PM||||Okay, Ill try that tonight. Will it be okay to just fill the system with plain water until I find and fix my leak? I will not be driving it at all until the leak is fixed. I was thinking about getting a pressurizer from J.C. Whitney, but they are like $80. I cant see spending that when Ill probally only use it once. I have other cars to drive so Im not in a huge hurry to get mine going again, but I do miss it.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||10-11-2000||05:34 PM||||There is no problem with filling the cooling system with plain water for the leak test.  Just don't leave it out where it could freeze.<P>Check to see if AutoZone or another parts house has a loaner pressure tester.  Sometimes they charge you a refundable fee to use the tool.  It's like a security deposit.<P>I never would have been able to find the leak in mine without the pressure tester.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||10-11-2000||08:02 PM||||I just got done refilling the system. I started the car with the cap off and made sure everything was working. I put the cap on and looked under the car, it was leaking. Not from coolant I spilled but leaking from the car. It was not pressurized and it will leak with pressure, but faster with. It all seem to run and concentrate on the clamp holding hoses on the pass. side, on the firewall in front of the A/c compressor. The big metal thing comming off the compressor, it seems to (maybe) come from on top of it, run down the edge and onto the hose the is touching it, not the hose connected to it, but the ones touching it and running under the car to the front, you know what Im talking about? I think my leak might be in the smaller hose going into the thermo housing but under the thermo, not that big hose on top, but that smaller one under it. Says like "To engine ????) I felt it and it is really hard, unlike the other softer hoses. You think this is my leak? Thanks so much for the help. I might just take it to a shop, I thought this would be a simple broken hose.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||10-12-2000||09:08 PM||||Would it possibly be my head gasket? I hope not, looks like a pain to replace. I cant really see good from under the car. You know that big metal container comming off the A/C compressor, how do I remove it from the compressor? The leak is comming from the top of it, and if I remove it, I might see where the leak is really comming from. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
